## What's happening?
The source buffer on `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}` stayed above 90% full for the full last 10 minutes, with a lowest reading of **{{value}}** of capacity. Capacity is 1,000 events per vCPU: once this reaches 1.0 the source blocks and inbound requests hang.
{{/is_alert}}{{#is_warning}}
## What's happening?
The source buffer on `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}` stayed above 70% full for the full last 10 minutes, with a lowest reading of **{{value}}** of capacity. Capacity is 1,000 events per vCPU, and headroom is shrinking.
{{/is_warning}}
{{#is_recovery}}The source buffer recovered for `{{component_id.name}}` in pipeline `{{pipeline_name.name}}`.{{/is_recovery}}

## If this monitor is quiet during a backpressure incident
A low ratio here is not an all-clear. The source channel being nearly empty while source blocked time is high means the constraint is **downstream of the channel**, not the channel itself. The source is not waiting for room in its own buffer; it is waiting for the next stage to accept work.

A ratio well under this threshold alongside multi-second blocked time is the signature of a processing stage that cannot drain, and the next place to look is `pipelines.buffer_send_duration_seconds` grouped by component, not this metric.

